# AI manifest — `public/.well-known/ai-manifest.json`

Every integratable publisher app ships a machine-readable **AI manifest** so the VTC Guide can discover and execute app APIs with the user's JWT.

Works for **both runtimes**: Next.js tools declare `http` (Guide POSTs to the app's server route); static Vite tools declare `zapi` (Guide executes against the app's ZAPI collections in-process — no server needed). Vite copies `public/` into `dist/`, so the manifest is served from static hosting unchanged.

## File location

```
public/.well-known/ai-manifest.json
```

Served at `https://{code}.apps.vtc.com/.well-known/ai-manifest.json` after deploy.

## Schema (v1)

```json
{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "module_code": "myapp",
  "description": "One-line purpose for semantic search",
  "tools": [
    {
      "name": "myapp.create_item",
      "description": "Create a record in this app",
      "parameters": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": { "name": { "type": "string" } },
        "required": ["name"]
      },
      "http": { "method": "POST", "path": "/api/items" },
      "permissions": ["member"],
      "examples": ["add a new contact", "create item Fix login"]
    }
  ]
}
```

| Field | Required | Notes |
|-------|----------|-------|
| `module_code` | yes | Same as app shortname |
| `description` | recommended | Embedded for orchestration / routing |
| `tools[].name` | yes | Prefer `{module_code}.{action}` |
| `tools[].http` | one of | Next.js: real app route — execution uses JWT query params |
| `tools[].zapi` | one of | Static Vite: collections target — see below |
| `tools[].permissions` | yes | `member`, `space_admin`, `network_admin` |
| `tools[].examples` | recommended | Natural-language phrases for embeddings |

**Alternate scaffold shape** (`app` wrapper, `endpoint` + `scopes`) is normalized at register time — prefer the canonical `http`/`zapi` + `permissions` form for new apps.

## `zapi` tools (static Vite apps)

Static apps have no server routes, so tools target the app's collections directly. ZAPI executes them in-process with the caller's JWT — same group DB, scoping, and access rules as the app's own `/collections/*` calls.

```json
{
  "name": "polls.add_poll",
  "description": "Create a poll",
  "parameters": { "type": "object", "properties": { "title": { "type": "string" } }, "required": ["title"] },
  "permissions": ["member"],
  "zapi": { "action": "create_document", "collection": "polls_items", "defaults": { "votes": 0 } }
}
```

| `zapi.action` | LLM arguments | Behavior |
|---------------|---------------|----------|
| `create_document` | business fields | Merged over `defaults`; stamps `created_by`, `group_id`, `status: active`, timestamps when absent |
| `list_documents` | `filter?`, `limit?` (≤50), `page?`, `sort_by?`, `sort_order?` | Equality-only filter (Mongo operators rejected); soft-deleted excluded; static `zapi.filter` merged on top |
| `get_document` | `document_id` | Fetch one |
| `update_document` | `document_id` + fields | Partial `$set` via atomic ops; bumps `updated_at` |
| `apply_ops` | `document_id`, `ops`, `only_if_absent?` | Atomic `inc`/`push`/`add_to_set`/`set` — race-free votes/counters (`nextjs-to-vite.mdc`) |

- `collection` must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$`; invalid `zapi` blocks fall back to `http` at normalize time — check registration output
- No business-logic validation runs server-side beyond `parameters` — only expose write tools whose invariants the actions above can enforce (use `only_if_absent` for uniqueness)

## Publish hook

After a successful deploy, **generate-app** (or CI) runs:

```bash
VTC_API_URL=https://api.vtc.com \
BUILD_INTERNAL_SECRET=... \
python scripts/register_app_ai_manifest.py {module_code} https://{code}.apps.vtc.com
```

This:

1. Fetches `/.well-known/ai-manifest.json`
2. `POST /api/v1/ai/manifests/register` (internal secret) → Mongo `ai_tool_manifests`
3. Syncs tool embeddings to vaultapi → Qdrant (`type: app_tool`)

## Runtime flow

1. **Semantic search** — Guide matches user intent to tools (installed apps only)
2. **Structured manifest** — OpenAI tool defs + permission checks from Mongo
3. **Execution** — `http` tools: `execution_engine` calls `{MODULE}_APP_URL` + path with `?token=&user=&group=` (`app-integration.mdc`); `zapi` tools: executed in-process against the caller's space collections

## Do

- Describe tools in plain language (names, descriptions, examples)
- List only routes the app actually implements
- Bump `schema_version` when tools change materially
- Keep write tools behind the same auth as normal `/api/*` routes

## Do not

- Embed secrets, admin-only routes, or raw OpenAPI dumps in the manifest
- Hardcode app URLs — execution resolves `{CODE}_APP_URL` from env on ZAPI
- Skip the manifest if the app has integratable write APIs

